/**
  @page examples-pdes-navierstokes Solving the Navier–Stokes equations
  @brief Follow the development of a vortex in a lid-driven cavity.

  The @ref examples-pdes-stokes "Stokes tutorial" introduces velocity–pressure
  coupling without inertia. Here the same cavity starts from rest, the lid is
  set in motion, and convection transports momentum through the fluid. The
  exported time series is used to produce the animation below.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-pdes-navierstokes-intro The mathematical problem

  On @f$\Omega=(0,1)^2@f$, the nondimensional incompressible equations are
  @f[
    \partial_tu+(u\cdot\nabla)u-\nu\Delta u+\nabla p=0,
    \qquad \operatorname{div}u=0.
  @f]
  Initially @f$u=0@f$. The bottom and side walls are stationary. On the top,
  @f[
    u(x,1,t)=\bigl(r(t)16x^2(1-x)^2,0\bigr),\qquad
    r(t)=\min(t,1).
  @f]
  The lid is ramped linearly over one time unit. Its final peak speed and the
  cavity width are both one. With @f$\nu=0.01@f$, the Reynolds number based on
  those scales is @f$\mathrm{Re}=100@f$.

  The lid profile vanishes at the corners, avoiding the conflicting corner
  values of a constant-speed lid. Comparisons with classical cavity reference
  data would require matching that boundary profile as well as the Reynolds
  number. Pressure is normalized by @f$\int_\Omega p\,dx=0@f$.

  @section examples-pdes-navierstokes-time The time discretization

  One Oseen solve is performed per time step: the transport velocity is taken
  from the previous step, while the transported velocity is implicit. With
  @f$\Delta t=0.05@f$,
  @f[
    \frac{u^{n+1}-u^n}{\Delta t}
      +(u^n\cdot\nabla)u^{n+1}-\nu\Delta u^{n+1}+\nabla p^{n+1}=0,
    \qquad \operatorname{div}u^{n+1}=0.
  @f]
  This is a first-order, semi-implicit approximation to Navier–Stokes. It does
  not perform a fully nonlinear Newton solve at every step. The 200 steps reach
  @f$t=10@f$; the final frame is a transient result, not a certified steady state.

  Multiplication by test velocity @f$v@f$ gives the momentum equation
  @f[
    \frac{1}{\Delta t}\int_\Omega u^{n+1}\cdot v\,dx
      +\int_\Omega\bigl((u^n\cdot\nabla)u^{n+1}\bigr)\cdot v\,dx
      +\nu\int_\Omega\nabla u^{n+1}:\nabla v\,dx
      -\int_\Omega p^{n+1}\operatorname{div}v\,dx
      =\frac{1}{\Delta t}\int_\Omega u^n\cdot v\,dx.
  @f]
  Continuity and the pressure-mean multiplier are imposed as in the Stokes
  example. Time-step and mesh refinement are needed to assess temporal and
  spatial error separately.

  @section examples-pdes-navierstokes-mesh 1. Prepare the cavity

  The same 512-triangle unit-square mesh and boundary labels are used as in
  the Stokes example.

  @snippet PDEs/NavierStokes.cpp navierstokes-mesh

  @section examples-pdes-navierstokes-fields 2. Initialize fields and output

  The Taylor–Hood pair uses quadratic velocity and linear pressure. The stored
  `velocity` holds the previous time step during assembly; after a successful
  solve, it is replaced with the new solution. The XDMF time series starts with
  the fluid at rest.

  @snippet PDEs/NavierStokes.cpp navierstokes-fields

  @section examples-pdes-navierstokes-step 3. Advance one step at a time

  `Mult(Jacobian(u), velocity)` expresses
  @f$(u^n\cdot\nabla)u^{n+1}@f$. The convection term makes the linear system
  nonsymmetric, so it is solved with sparse LU rather than CG. Each time step
  checks solver success before updating or writing the fields.

  @snippet PDEs/NavierStokes.cpp navierstokes-step

  The form is built with the current previous-step field and lid value. There
  is no additional nonlinear iteration hidden inside this loop.

  @section examples-pdes-navierstokes-output 4. Watch the vortex develop

  @htmlonly
  <figure class="m-figure rodin-tutorial-video">
    <video controls playsinline preload="metadata" poster="NavierStokes.png"
           aria-label="Computed lid-driven cavity flow at Reynolds number 100">
      <source src="NavierStokes.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
      <a href="NavierStokes.mp4">Watch the cavity animation</a>
    </video>
    <figcaption>Computed speed and instantaneous streamlines from rest to time 10.
      The lid drives a clockwise recirculation; the displayed time is simulation time.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  @endhtmlonly

  The right panel makes the primary vortex visible without relying on a color
  gradient alone. These are instantaneous streamlines, not particle trajectories
  accumulated through the transient flow. They are drawn from an interpolation
  of the exported velocity samples. No analytic or illustrative flow is added
  to the computed result.

  Open `NavierStokes.xdmf` in ParaView and select **Apply**. Color by the magnitude
  of **velocity**, then use the time controls to inspect the evolution. Add the <strong>Stream Tracer</strong> filter to examine circulation and color by **pressure** to inspect
  the pressure field. Keep all referenced HDF5 snapshots next to the XDMF file.

  The optional `examples/PDEs/visualize.py` script produces the poster and MP4
  from the same time series. A still image of the final frame is also available:

  @image html PDEs/NavierStokes.png "Velocity magnitude and clockwise circulation at time 10." width=720px

  @section examples-pdes-navierstokes-full Complete program

  @snippet PDEs/NavierStokes.cpp navierstokes-source

  See @ref PDEs/NavierStokes.cpp "NavierStokes.cpp" for this native example.
  A separate [PETSc SNES example](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_NavierStokes.cpp)
  demonstrates a fully implicit nonlinear formulation with both Newton convection
  terms. Its time integrator and nonlinear solve differ from the Oseen scheme
  presented here; see the @ref guides-petsc "PETSc guide" for that backend.

  @example PDEs/NavierStokes.cpp @m_examplenavigation{examples-pdes-navierstokes,PDEs/} @m_footernavigation
*/
